window.ELABORATE = {};

function addNotes(year, topic, notes) {
  window.ELABORATE[year + ":" + topic] = notes;
}

addNotes("7", "S.1", [
  "Scientists choose apparatus so the measurement matches the quantity. Volume, mass, temperature, force and length all need different tools.\nKEYWORD: apparatus — the equipment you use in an experiment.\nA mismatch (using a beaker as if it were a measuring cylinder) adds avoidable error before you have even started.\nTypical glassware: beakers for mixing, conical flasks for swirling, test tubes for small samples, boiling tubes when you need more volume or stronger heating.\nKEYWORD: spatula — transfers solids.\nKEYWORD: dropper pipette — transfers small volumes of liquid.\nA heatproof mat, eye protection and a stable tripod matter as much as reading the scale correctly.",
  "A measuring cylinder is marked more finely than a beaker, so 50 cm³ is closer to the true volume. Choose a cylinder whose full-scale volume is not huge compared with 50 cm³. A 100 cm³ cylinder is usually better than a 1000 cm³ one.\nKEYWORD: measuring cylinder — glass or plastic tube with a fine scale for volume.\nBeaker lines are manufacturing guides, not precision scales. Use them only for approximate amounts.",
  "Water in glass forms a concave meniscus. Your eye must be level with the liquid so you do not read high or low.\nKEYWORD: meniscus — the curved surface of a liquid in a tube.\nKEYWORD: parallax — a wrong reading because your eye is not level with the scale.\nRead the bottom of the water curve against the nearest scale division. For a mercury thermometer the meniscus is convex, so you would read the top.",
  "Grinding increases surface area. Solute particles then meet solvent particles more often, so dissolving or a surface reaction speeds up.\nKEYWORD: pestle — the club-shaped tool.\nKEYWORD: mortar — the bowl you grind in.\nDo not grind explosively reactive or very hard materials without instruction.",
  "A Bunsen burner needs a heatproof mat. A tripod and gauze support a beaker in the flame. A boss, clamp and stand can hold a boiling tube at a safe angle.\nNever heat a sealed container. Point the mouth of a tube away from people.",
]);

addNotes("7", "S.2", [
  "KEYWORD: accuracy — how close a reading is to the true value.\nKEYWORD: precision — how closely repeats agree with each other.\nYou can be precise but inaccurate (a badly zeroed balance that always reads 2 g high). Think about the instrument’s range and smallest scale division, how you use it, and whether the method introduces delay or parallax.",
  "Place the ruler’s true zero against one end. Many rulers have a worn strip before the zero. Keep the scale alongside the object, not at an angle. For a leaf or wire, measure the same feature each time.",
  "Repeats let you calculate a mean, which reduces the effect of random error. They also reveal a reading that does not fit.\nKEYWORD: mean — the average of your repeats.\nThree sensible repeats are a common school minimum. Write the raw values, not only the mean.",
  "Human reaction time is often 0.2–0.3 s. For a toy car covering 1 m in a fraction of a second, that error is large.\nKEYWORD: light gate — starts and stops a timer when a beam is broken.\nA wall clock is worse, because you cannot read it to tenths of a second reliably.",
]);

addNotes("7", "S.3", [
  "KEYWORD: anomalous result — a reading that does not follow the pattern of the rest of the data.\nIt might be a misread scale, a stopwatch started late, a sample that was not stirred, or a one-off contamination. Do not quietly delete data without comment. In an evaluation you explain what you think went wrong.",
  "The best response is to measure again under the same conditions. If that is impossible, omit the odd value from the mean and say so. Keep it in the table so the reader can see it. Never invent a ‘nice’ number to replace it.",
  "On a graph an outlier sits off the line of best fit. Draw the line through the trend, not forced through the odd point. Then think: did the thermometer stick, was an ice cube not fully melted, was a bubble count missed?",
]);

addNotes("7", "S.4", [
  "Units are a memory aid. Metres per second already says distance divided by time. If a quantity is a product in real life, the formula multiplies. Always check that the units on both sides match after you substitute numbers.",
  "The three forms are rearrangements of one idea.\nFORMULA: speed = distance ÷ time\nFORMULA: distance = speed × time\nFORMULA: time = distance ÷ speed\nCover the quantity you want in a formula triangle if that helps, but also say the meaning in words.",
  "Worked example: a train moves at 75 m/s and takes 3.6 s to pass an observer. Length is a distance.\nFORMULA: length = speed × time = 75 × 3.6 = 270 m\nInclude units in the answer. If time were in minutes you would convert to seconds first when speed is in m/s.",
]);

addNotes("7", "1.1", [
  "KEYWORD: respiratory system — the organs that carry air between the outside world and the blood.\nOxygen must reach cells for aerobic respiration. Carbon dioxide must leave or it would change the blood and make you breathless.\nKEYWORD: breathing (ventilation) — moving air in and out of the lungs. It is only the first step.\nGas exchange happens at the air sacs. Respiration itself happens in cells all over the body, not only in the lungs.",
  "Air usually enters through the nose, where it is warmed, moistened and partly cleaned, or through the mouth. It passes the larynx, then the trachea, which splits into two bronchi, one to each lung. These divide into narrower bronchioles that end in clusters of alveoli.\nKEYWORD: larynx — voice box at the top of the trachea.\nKEYWORD: trachea — windpipe. C-shaped cartilage rings hold it open.\nKEYWORD: bronchi — the two branches from the trachea into each lung.\nKEYWORD: bronchioles — narrower tubes that end at the alveoli.\nKEYWORD: alveoli — air sacs where gases are exchanged.\nMucus and cilia trap dust and microbes and move them up and out. Smoking damages this cleaning system.",
  "C-shaped cartilage rings hold the trachea open so it does not flatten when you inhale. The gap in the C faces the oesophagus so food can pass down behind the windpipe. Without those rings, the soft tube would collapse like a drinking straw sucked too hard.",
  "The larynx sits at the top of the trachea. Stretched vocal folds vibrate as air passes, producing sound.\nKEYWORD: epiglottis — flap that keeps food out of the trachea during swallowing.",
  "People say ‘respiration’ when they mean breathing, but in biology the words are distinct.\nKEYWORD: ventilation — moving air.\nKEYWORD: gas exchange — swapping O₂ and CO₂ at the alveoli.\nKEYWORD: respiration — chemical release of energy from food, mainly in mitochondria, using oxygen if it is aerobic.\nPlants and animals both respire. Only some tissues photosynthesise.",
]);

addNotes("7", "1.2", [
  "In each alveolus, oxygen is at a higher concentration in the air than in the blood arriving from the body, so O₂ diffuses into the blood and binds to haemoglobin in red blood cells. Carbon dioxide is more concentrated in that blood, so it diffuses the other way and is breathed out.\nThe process is passive. A good blood flow and ventilation keep the gradients steep.",
  "Millions of alveoli give a huge surface. Walls are one cell thick, so the diffusion path is short. Each sac is wrapped in capillaries. Moisture lets gases dissolve before they cross. Disease that thickens walls or fills sacs with fluid slows exchange.",
  "KEYWORD: diffusion — the net movement of particles from higher concentration to lower concentration, down a concentration gradient.\nIt happens in liquids and gases because particles move randomly. In lungs we care about oxygen, carbon dioxide and water vapour.",
  "During exercise, muscle cells respire faster. They take more oxygen from the blood and produce more carbon dioxide. Sensors detect the CO₂ rise. The brain increases breathing rate and depth, and the heart pumps faster.\nThat is why you pant after a sprint — the body is restoring oxygen and clearing CO₂.",
]);

addNotes("7", "1.3", [
  "Air is not sucked in by a vacuum pump in the mouth. The thorax is made larger by muscle action. Pressure of the air already in the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure, so outside air is pushed in.\nKEYWORD: thorax — the chest cavity that holds the lungs.\nExhaling is the reverse: volume falls, pressure rises, air is pushed out.",
  "Inhalation: the diaphragm contracts and flattens. External intercostal muscles lift and expand the ribcage. The lungs follow that volume increase.\nKEYWORD: diaphragm — a sheet of muscle under the lungs.\nKEYWORD: intercostal muscles — muscles between the ribs.",
  "Exhalation at rest is largely passive: diaphragm and intercostals relax, the ribcage drops, volume decreases. In forced breathing, internal intercostals and abdominal muscles actively push air out faster. Asthma narrows bronchioles, so moving that volume takes more effort.",
  "No new substances are made when you inhale or exhale. Air is just moved.\nFORMULA: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water (+ energy)\nRespiration in cells is that chemical reaction. Mixing the two words loses marks.",
]);

addNotes("7", "1.4", [
  "KEYWORD: aerobic respiration — how most of your cells release usable energy in a controlled way, using oxygen.\nFORMULA: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water (+ energy)\nIt is not the same as breathing, and it is not only an animal process.",
  "KEYWORD: mitochondria — the main site of the oxygen-using stages of respiration.\nCells that work hard (muscle, sperm, some plant tissues) are packed with them. The first steps of breaking glucose can occur in the cytoplasm, but the large energy yield needs mitochondria and oxygen.",
  "Every living cell respires, including plant cells in the dark and in the light. In daylight, photosynthesis in green cells may take in more CO₂ than respiration produces, so a plant can appear to ‘only photosynthesise’ if you only measure net gas exchange. Seeds, roots and night-time leaves clearly show plant respiration.",
  "Energy from respiration drives muscle contraction, active transport, building proteins, cell division, and in birds and mammals the heat that keeps body temperature stable. If respiration fails, those processes stop.",
]);

addNotes("7", "2.1", [
  "When a soluble solid dissolves, its particles separate and spread evenly through the liquid. The mixture is a solution: clear if fully dissolved. The solid can often be recovered by evaporating the solvent. Nothing is destroyed. Mass is conserved.",
  "KEYWORD: solute — the substance being dissolved.\nKEYWORD: solvent — the liquid that does the dissolving (often water).\nKEYWORD: solution — solute + solvent mixed uniformly.\nKEYWORD: aqueous — the solvent is water.\nAir is a solution of gases. Brass is a solid solution of metals (an alloy).",
  "KEYWORD: melting — a change of state caused by heating until particles can slide. No second substance is required.\nKEYWORD: dissolving — needs a solvent and can happen at room temperature.\nIce melting in a drink is melting. Sugar disappearing in the same drink is dissolving.",
  "Crushing increases surface area. Stirring moves dissolved particles away so fresh solvent meets undissolved solid. Warming usually makes solvent particles move faster, so collisions that pull solute into solution happen more often. A whole sugar cube in cold still tea dissolves slowly; stirred hot tea is much faster.",
]);

addNotes("7", "2.2", [
  "KEYWORD: solubility — how many grams of solute dissolve in a stated mass or volume of solvent at a stated temperature, often g per 100 g of water.\nIt is a property of that solute–solvent pair, not a vague ‘it dissolves well’.",
  "KEYWORD: saturated solution — holds as much solute as it can at that temperature. Extra crystals sit on the bottom.\nKEYWORD: unsaturated solution — can still take more solute.\nIf you heat a saturated solution, more may dissolve. If you cool a hot saturated solution, crystals often appear.",
  "For many ionic solids, solubility rises with temperature. Gases are the opposite: a warm fizzy drink goes flat faster because gas solubility falls as temperature rises. Always name the solute and the solvent when you quote a trend.",
  "A figure such as ‘36 g per 100 g of water’ is incomplete without the temperature. State the unit as well as the number.",
]);

addNotes("7", "2.3", [
  "KEYWORD: independent variable — the one you change on purpose (here, temperature).\nKEYWORD: dependent variable — what you measure as a result (mass of solute that dissolves, or time to dissolve).\nA good plan names both, with units, before you start heating anything.",
  "KEYWORD: control variables — what you keep the same so the test is fair.\nKeep the same volume or mass of distilled water, the same solute, similar crystal size, the same stirring method and time, and the same criterion for ‘dissolved’. If two things change at once you cannot say which caused the result.",
  "Eye protection, care with hot water and clamps, and a sensible temperature range are part of a real plan. Repeats at each temperature, recorded in a table, let you average and spot anomalies.",
  "A teaspoon is not a unit. Mass on a balance is comparable between groups. You still need the mass of dry solute or of water used so solubility can be calculated.",
]);

addNotes("7", "2.4", [
  "KEYWORD: paper chromatography — separates dissolved dyes because they spend different times stuck to the paper versus dissolved in the moving solvent. Faster travellers end up higher up.\nIt is used to compare inks, food colourings and plant pigments.",
  "A pencil start line does not dissolve. Pen ink would run and confuse the chromatogram. Spots must start above the solvent so they are carried up, not washed into the beaker. A lid reduces evaporation so the solvent front climbs evenly.",
  "FORMULA: Rf = distance from start line to spot ÷ distance from start line to solvent front\nRf has no unit and is always less than 1. Same Rf in the same solvent suggests the same substance, but it is evidence, not a proof on its own.",
  "A pure dye usually gives one spot. A mixture gives more, unless two dyes travel together. Comparing unknown spots with known pure dyes side by side on the same paper is more convincing than Rf alone.",
]);

addNotes("7", "3.1", [
  "KEYWORD: force — a push or a pull. It can change an object’s speed, direction or shape.\nTypes you meet: gravity (weight), friction, drag, tension, reaction from a surface, magnetic and electrostatic forces.\nForces are vectors: they have size and direction. Arrows on diagrams should be labelled in newtons.",
  "KEYWORD: resultant force — the single force that could replace all the forces acting.\nIf resultants along a line add to zero, the object stays at rest or keeps constant velocity in a straight line. Unbalanced resultant means acceleration in that direction.",
  "KEYWORD: newton (N) — the unit of force.\nKEYWORD: weight — the gravitational force on a mass, about 10 N for each kilogram on Earth.\nA forcemeter contains a spring. Mass in kilograms is not the same as weight in newtons.",
  "Constant speed in a straight line means driving forces balance resistive forces. The engine still does work against friction, but speed is not changing. At steady speed on the level, the forces are equal.",
]);

addNotes("7", "3.2", [
  "KEYWORD: speed — distance travelled per unit time.\nKEYWORD: average speed — total distance ÷ total time for the whole journey.\nKEYWORD: instantaneous speed — the speed at one moment (a speedometer reading).",
  "The SI unit is metres per second (m/s). Road signs use kilometres per hour (km/h).\nFORMULA: 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h\nAlways write the unit beside the number.",
  "Example: 240 m in 30 s.\nFORMULA: average speed = 240 ÷ 30 = 8 m/s\nShow the substitution, not only 8. If the cyclist stopped, include the stop in the total time.",
  "FORMULA: distance = speed × time\nUse this when speed is constant, or when you use average speed. 12 m/s for 15 s → 180 m. Convert minutes to seconds if speed is per second.",
]);

addNotes("7", "3.3", [
  "On a distance–time graph, gradient = speed. A steeper straight line is faster steady speed. Horizontal means stationary. Returning toward the time axis means moving back toward the start.\nFORMULA: speed = change in distance ÷ change in time",
  "KEYWORD: velocity — speed in a stated direction. 5 m/s east is not the same velocity as 5 m/s west.\nA car around a roundabout at constant speed still has changing velocity because direction changes, so it is accelerating.",
  "KEYWORD: acceleration — change of velocity per second, in metres per second squared (m/s²).\nSlowing down is deceleration. A force is required to accelerate, including to turn a corner.",
  "A curve on a distance–time graph means the gradient is changing, so speed is changing. Getting steeper means speeding up; flattening means slowing. Do not call a straight slope ‘accelerating’.",
]);

addNotes("7", "3.4", [
  "KEYWORD: moment — the turning effect of a force.\nFORMULA: moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot\nA small force far from a hinge can match a large force close in. Spanners, see-saws, doors and crowbars are all moment problems.",
  "The unit is the newton-metre (Nm). Always use the perpendicular distance, not a slant distance along a beam if the force is not at right angles.",
  "KEYWORD: principle of moments — for a balanced object, total clockwise moment equals total anticlockwise moment about the same pivot.\nThat is how you find an unknown weight or an unknown distance on a see-saw.",
  "20 N at 0.5 m.\nFORMULA: moment = 20 × 0.5 = 10 Nm\nAt this stage, questions usually keep the force at 90° to the beam.",
]);

addNotes("7", "3.5", [
  "KEYWORD: pressure — how concentrated a force is on a surface.\nFORMULA: pressure = force ÷ area\nSame force on a tiny area (stiletto heel) gives huge pressure. On a snowshoe it is small. That is why drawing pins go in point-first.",
  "KEYWORD: pascal (Pa) — the same as N/m².\nSchool calculations sometimes use N/cm². Convert cm² to m² if the unit required is Pa: divide area in cm² by 10 000.",
  "A sharp knife has a small edge area, so the same hand force produces enough pressure to separate food. A blunt knife spreads force and crushes more than it cuts.",
  "Camels, skis and desert tyres increase area. Pressure on sand or snow falls, so you are less likely to sink.",
]);

addNotes("7", "3.6", [
  "Liquid pressure increases with depth because a deeper point supports a taller column of liquid. Divers feel this in their ears. Dams are thicker at the base. Pressure also depends on the liquid’s density.",
  "Pressure in a fluid at a given depth acts equally in all directions. That is why a hole in a bottle squirts outward, and why hydraulic machines can send force around corners through pipes.",
  "The atmosphere has weight. At sea level this is about 100 000 Pa (1 atmosphere). Higher up there is less air above you, so pressure falls. That is why aircraft cabins are pressurised and why water boils at a lower temperature on a mountain.",
  "As a plane climbs or descends, outside pressure changes quickly. Air in the middle ear is slower to equalise, so the eardrum flexes until you swallow or yawn and the pressures match.",
]);

addNotes("7", "3.7", [
  "KEYWORD: diffusion — particles spreading from where they are crowded to where they are less crowded.\nA gas is mostly empty space with particles moving freely and colliding. Pressure on a wall is those collisions. More particles, faster particles, or a smaller container all raise pressure.",
  "In a sealed can of fixed volume, heating raises particle kinetic energy. Hits on the walls are harder and more frequent, so pressure rises. This is why aerosol cans warn against fire and heat.",
  "When you push a pump piston, you do work on the air. Particle kinetic energy increases, so temperature rises. The barrel feels warm.",
  "Overnight cooling slows the air particles in a tyre. If the volume barely changes, pressure drops and the tyre looks slightly flatter. The air has not leaked in large amounts.",
]);

addNotes("7", "4.1", [
  "Picture the Sonoran Desert: saguaro cactus, Gila woodpecker, cactus wren, kangaroo rat, ants, bats and a tarantula.\nKEYWORD: interact — living things affect each other and the non-living world around them.\nThe woodpecker drills a nest hole in the saguaro. Later another bird may use the hole. A cactus wren nests in teddy-bear cholla; the spines keep snakes away. Bats drink agave nectar and carry pollen. Ants store seeds.\nKEYWORD: pollinating — moving pollen so a plant can make seeds.\nKEYWORD: nectar — sugary liquid in a flower that bats and insects drink.\nKEYWORD: pollen — fine grains a flower makes so it can reproduce.",
  "Living things also need the non-living environment.\nKEYWORD: environment — everything around an organism, living and non-living.\nLight: saguaro flowers open at night so bats can reach them.\nKEYWORD: nocturnal — active at night. A kangaroo rat stays in a cool burrow by day.\nSoil: plants grow in it; a tarantula uses a burrow.\nWater keeps cells alive. After rain, desert life becomes more active.\nAir: plants use carbon dioxide; animals use oxygen. Kangaroo-rat droppings become part of the soil.\nKEYWORD: adaptations — features that help a species survive in its habitat (spines, water storage, night activity).",
  "KEYWORD: ecosystem — a network of interactions between living organisms and the non-living things around them.\nKEYWORD: food web — feeding links. Plants are producers (sunlight → food). Energy then passes to grasshoppers, kangaroo rats, lizards, rattlesnakes and a red-tailed hawk.\nThe arrows point to the eater (energy flow).\nNot every interaction is feeding: roots hold soil; animals pollinate flowers.\nKEYWORD: habitat — the place a species naturally lives. A woodpecker’s habitat is the saguaro plus the open desert where it finds food. Termites live at the cactus base; sap beetles live inside flowers.\nKEYWORD: ecology — the study of ecosystems.\nAn ecosystem is bigger than one habitat: it is all the habitats plus all the interactions.",
]);

addNotes("7", "4.2", [
  "Marcus said an ecosystem is a place. Sofia said it is more than a place. Sofia is right: it is the interactions.\nKEYWORD: habitat — the place a species naturally lives inside an ecosystem.\nMangrove forests: trees with roots in seawater. Young fish hide among the roots. Mudskippers climb onto mud at low tide. Leaves rot; prawns and crabs eat them. Crab-eating macaques hunt crabs on the roots.",
  "Arctic sea ice: seawater freezes in winter. Seals hunt fish but must surface to breathe. Polar bears walk from floe to floe. Arctic foxes hunt on the ice.\nEnough light reaches the underside of ice for tiny algae (producers). Shrimp-like animals eat the algae; fish eat the shrimps.\nFORMULA: algae → shrimps → fish → polar bear (and fox)\nDifferent climate means different habitats and species, but the same idea as the desert food web.",
  "A rice paddy in Malaysia is a farmed ecosystem. Fields flood. Algae grow on mud. Fish swim in from irrigation canals. Frogs and dragonflies breed. Shallow water heats by day and cools at night. Fertilizer helps rice and algae, so animals get more food. Birds feed around the field.\nYou can study a garden or pond the same way: list habitats, then look for feeding, hiding, pollinating and decomposing.\nIf you catch an animal, release it exactly where you found it.",
]);

addNotes("7", "4.3", [
  "Imagine someone planting a cactus that grows faster than the saguaro. Native cacti and the birds that nest in them could lose food and nest sites.\nKEYWORD: native species — a species that belongs in that ecosystem naturally.\nNew Zealand split from other land about 66 million years ago. Before humans there were no predatory land mammals. Many birds nested on the ground. The kiwi never needed to fly.",
  "Humans arrived about 700 years ago and brought animals that did not belong. Rats hid on boats and ate eggs and chicks. Sheep were farmed. Rabbits escaped and ate grass, so stoats were brought from Europe to hunt rabbits.\nStoats spread and breed quickly. They made birds such as the laughing owl and New Zealand thrush extinct and eat about 60% of kiwi chicks.\nKEYWORD: extinct — none of that species left alive.\nKEYWORD: eradicate — completely get rid of. People try to eradicate stoats, but it is very hard once a species has settled.\nKEYWORD: invasive species — a species brought in that spreads and harms the species already there.\nAbout 53 native bird species have become extinct since humans arrived, mostly because of invasive species.",
  "Buffelgrass is native to Africa, Asia and the Middle East. It was planted in Arizona as cattle food. Now it spreads in dense patches and takes water and nutrients from Sonoran soil. Native plants and the animals that use them can suffer.\nStoats are not a ‘problem animal’ in Europe, because their prey evolved with them. In New Zealand, flightless birds had never met a mammal hunter.",
]);

addNotes("7", "4.4", [
  "KEYWORD: insecticide — a chemical that kills insects.\nDDT was made in the 1940s to kill mosquitoes (malaria, typhus) and crop pests. People even stood in the spray. In 1962 Rachel Carson’s book Silent Spring showed that birds were dying too.\nKEYWORD: persistent — does not break down for many years. Decomposers cannot digest DDT. Spray can blow far away. Once it is in a body it stays for life.\nKEYWORD: toxic — poisonous. In birds it makes eggshells thin, so chicks never hatch.",
  "A class game models a food chain. Red tokens stand for DDT. Insects pick up a few. Small birds eat many insects. One eagle eats many small birds — so the eagle’s bag has the most red tokens.\nKEYWORD: accumulate — build up.\nKEYWORD: bioaccumulation — all the poison an organism takes in over its life stays and adds together in its body.\nFORMULA: algae → shrimps → fish → cormorant\nEvery shrimp a fish eats adds more DDT.",
  "KEYWORD: biomagnification — the concentration of poison gets larger as you go up the food chain.\nppm means grams of DDT in one million grams of organism.\nFORMULA: water 0.00005 ppm → protists 0.04 ppm → shrimps 0.16 ppm → minnows 0.50 ppm → cormorant 26.40 ppm\nThe cormorant has about 50 times as much as a minnow because it eats many minnows.\nKEYWORD: biodegradable — can be broken down by microbes or inside a body, so it is less likely to magnify.",
]);

addNotes("7", "5.1", [
  "KEYWORD: atom — the smallest piece of an element that is still that element.\nKEYWORD: proton — positive charge, in the nucleus.\nKEYWORD: neutron — no charge, in the nucleus.\nKEYWORD: electron — negative charge, moving around the nucleus.\nHelium has two protons, two neutrons and two electrons. Almost all the mass is in the nucleus. Opposite charges attract, so electrostatic attraction holds the atom together. Most of the volume is empty space.",
  "Thomson found the electron (1890s) and pictured a plum pudding. Rutherford fired particles at gold foil: most went straight through, a few bounced back — so atoms are mostly empty space with a tiny dense nucleus. He found the proton. Chadwick found the neutron in 1932. Scientists collaborate and use peer review. Models change when new evidence arrives.",
  "KEYWORD: proton number — decides the element (carbon always has 6).\nKEYWORD: mass number — protons + neutrons.\nFORMULA: mass number = protons + neutrons\nAn atom with 8 protons and 8 neutrons has mass number 16.",
]);

addNotes("7", "5.2", [
  "KEYWORD: pure — one substance. A pure element has only one type of atom.\n24 carat gold is pure gold. 18 carat is 18/24 × 100 = 75% gold.\nFORMULA: 18 carat = 18/24 × 100 = 75% gold\nSilver 925 is 92.5% silver. Pure diamond is only carbon; nitrogen can make it yellow, boron blue.",
  "Seawater is about 96.5% water and 3.5% mixed salts. Only about 68% of those salts is sodium chloride, so evaporated sea salt is not pure NaCl.\nFORMULA: 1000 g seawater → about 35 g salts → about 24 g NaCl\nMedicines often need extra purification because reactions can make more than one product.",
]);

addNotes("7", "5.3", [
  "KEYWORD: weather — the atmosphere now: temperature, humidity, rain, cloud, visibility, wind. It can change in minutes.\nKEYWORD: climate — the average weather over many years (usually more than 30).\nA cold week is weather, not proof about climate.\nKEYWORD: meteorology — the study of weather.\nKEYWORD: climatology — the study of climate.\nMeasure at the same time each day. Take temperature in the shade.",
  "Climate zones depend on temperature and precipitation: polar, temperate, arid, tropical, Mediterranean, mountains/tundra. A Mediterranean climate can exist far from the Mediterranean Sea.",
]);

addNotes("7", "5.4", [
  "About 25 000 years ago ice sheets covered much more of Earth than today.\nKEYWORD: glacial — ice far from the poles.\nKEYWORD: interglacial — ice mainly at the poles (that is now).\nKEYWORD: ice age — a longer cold chapter with many glacials and interglacials inside it. There have been several ice ages.",
  "Evidence: boulders dumped when glaciers melted, scratches on rock, pollen in peat-bog cores, frozen mammoths, fossils of cold-climate plants and animals in places that are warm now.",
]);

addNotes("7", "5.5", [
  "Early atmosphere came from volcanoes: mostly carbon dioxide, almost no oxygen, surface hotter than 400 °C. Plants and microbes used CO₂ and released oxygen.\nFORMULA: today’s dry air ≈ 78% nitrogen + 21% oxygen",
  "Burning fossil fuels, deforestation and making cement release CO₂ that had been locked in coal, oil and limestone.\nFORMULA: carbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide\nGreenhouse gases act like a blanket: extra CO₂ traps more heat, so the surface tends to warm.",
]);

addNotes("7", "6.1", [
  "Light travels in straight lines called rays. When a ray hits a shiny flat surface it bounces.\nKEYWORD: reflection — light bouncing off a surface.\nKEYWORD: plane mirror — a flat mirror.\nKEYWORD: incident ray — the ray coming in from the lamp or ray box.\nKEYWORD: reflected ray — the ray that bounces off.\nKEYWORD: normal — a dashed guide line drawn at 90° (perpendicular) to the mirror at the bounce point.\nFORMULA: angle of incidence (i) = angle of reflection (r)\nBoth angles are measured from the normal, never from the glass.",
  "In the book practical you use a ray box, a plane mirror, a protractor and a set square.\n1. Draw the mirror line and stand the plane mirror on it.\n2. Use the set square so the normal is truly perpendicular.\n3. Put the protractor centre on the bounce point, zero on the normal, then read i and r.\nIf the ray hits along the normal, i = 0°, so r = 0° and it bounces straight back.",
  "Motorcycle and car wing mirrors are plane mirrors. Light from a car behind hits the glass and reflects into the rider’s eyes, so they can see traffic without turning around.\nA bathroom plane mirror is smooth, so you see a sharp face. Crumpled kitchen foil is still shiny, but each crease has a different normal, so rays scatter and the face disappears.",
]);

addNotes("7", "6.2", [
  "Light does not travel at the same speed in every material.\nFORMULA: speed in air ≈ 300 000 km/s\nFORMULA: speed in water ≈ 225 000 km/s\nFORMULA: speed in glass ≈ 200 000 km/s\nKEYWORD: refraction — a change of direction when light changes speed.\nThink of roller-skates hitting a patch of sand at an angle: the first wheel slows, so you turn.",
  "FORMULA: air → glass or water: ray bends towards the normal\nFORMULA: glass or water → air: ray bends away from the normal\nIf the ray meets the surface along the normal it slows or speeds up but does not bend.\nLenses in glasses and in a phone camera are shaped pieces of glass or plastic that use refraction to form a clear picture. A wet window looks wavy because water and glass both slow the light.",
  "A straight pencil in a glass of water looks broken at the water line. Rays from the underwater part leave water (slower) into air (faster) and bend away from the normal. Your brain traces them as straight lines, so that part looks shifted.\nAppearing coin: a coin on the bottom of a bowl can hide behind the rim. Pour in water and the coin seems to rise — refraction now sends rays into your eye.\nOil on water adds a second boundary, so the beam bends twice.",
]);

addNotes("7", "6.3", [
  "In 1666 Newton shone sunlight through a triangular glass prism. White light is not one colour.\nKEYWORD: dispersion — white light splitting into its colours.\nKEYWORD: spectrum — the spread of colours you see.\nKEYWORD: prism — a triangular block of glass (or plastic) used to disperse light.\nThe order is red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.",
  "Each colour travels at a slightly different speed in glass, so each colour refracts by a different amount.\nKEYWORD: red — refracted through the smallest angle.\nKEYWORD: violet — refracted through the largest angle.\nIn the book practical a ray box, a triangular prism and a white card screen make a spectrum. Move the screen closer and the colours pack together; move it further and they spread.",
  "A rainbow needs three things:\n• the Sun shining (bright light)\n• rain or spray in the air (drops that disperse the light)\n• the Sun behind you, because each drop also reflects light inside towards you.\nA firefighter’s hose on a sunny day can make the same arch. You do not see a rainbow at night because there is no sunlight.",
]);

addNotes("7", "6.4", [
  "White light has the seven rainbow colours, but you can make every screen colour from three primaries of light.\nKEYWORD: primary colours of light — red, green and blue. You cannot make these by mixing other colours of light.\nThey are not the same as paint primaries. Light mixes by adding.\nFORMULA: red + green = yellow\nFORMULA: red + blue = magenta\nFORMULA: blue + green = cyan\nFORMULA: red + green + blue = white\nA phone or television picture is tiny red, green and blue sources. Orange is mostly red plus a little green.",
  "KEYWORD: coloured filter — a transparent sheet that lets some colours through and removes others.\nKEYWORD: transmit — get through.\nKEYWORD: absorb — taken in and not passed on.\nKEYWORD: subtraction — colours removed from white light.\nTraffic lights use three identical white lamps. A red, yellow or green filter sits in front of each one. A red filter transmits red and absorbs the other six spectrum colours.\nA green filter then a red filter: only green gets through the first, and the red filter absorbs that green, so the end result is no light (black).",
  "You see an object’s colour because of the light it reflects.\nA yellow flower reflects yellow and absorbs the rest.\nA white ball reflects all the colours that shine on it.\nA black ball absorbs them, so almost nothing comes back.\nA red cube in green light looks black: it can only reflect red, and there is no red to bounce. A blue cube in red light looks black for the same reason.\nA red T-shirt can look red in white light or in red light. In green light it looks dark.",
]);

addNotes("7", "6.5", [
  "On a dark night, far from street lights, you can see a milky band. That band is part of our own galaxy.\nKEYWORD: galaxy — dust, gas, stars and solar systems held together by gravity.\nKEYWORD: Milky Way — the spiral galaxy we live in. The name comes from a Greek word for milk.\nWe sit inside the disc, so we see one ‘arm’ as a band. From far away the Milky Way would look like a spiral. About 250 000 000 000 stars, including our Sun.",
  "Other galaxies have other shapes.\nKEYWORD: spiral — pinwheel arms (Milky Way from far away).\nKEYWORD: elliptical — a smooth oval.\nKEYWORD: irregular — a patchy, uneven cloud.\nThe closest known galaxy is the Canis Major Dwarf Galaxy. It is elliptical.\nKEYWORD: universe — all of space and everything in it.\nSize order from the book starter: moon → planet → star → solar system → galaxy → universe.",
  "KEYWORD: stellar dust — the dust found in space. A cloud of it may have only about one particle in every million cubic metres.\nGravity is strong because a galaxy has huge mass. Even at 300 000 km/s it would take more than 100 000 years to cross the Milky Way. Its mass is about 1 500 000 000 000 times the Sun.\nScientists counted galaxies in one patch of sky and scaled up. One estimate is 100 000 000 000 galaxies. That is the same idea as counting sand grains in a cup, then using mass or volume to estimate a whole bucket.",
]);

addNotes("7", "6.6", [
  "KEYWORD: asteroid — a rock that orbits the Sun. Asteroids are smaller than planets. Scientists think they are leftover rock from when the Solar System formed.\nMost are not round. The book describes the usual shape as like a potato.\nKEYWORD: asteroid belt — the region between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter where most asteroids travel. There are millions of them.",
  "The largest asteroid is Ceres, found in 1801. It is about 975 km across, almost round, and covered in craters. It has a core, mantle and crust. Scientists think it might have become a planet if it had kept growing.\nThe smallest asteroid that has been studied is only about 2 m across.\nItokawa is a small potato-shaped asteroid, about 530 m long and 250 m wide. In 2005 a spacecraft collected samples. It looks like lumps of rock stuck together after impacts. Gravity is weak, so a close pass by a planet can change its shape.",
  "KEYWORD: crater — a bowl-shaped scar from a collision.\nKEYWORD: impact — a high-speed collision.\nEarth pulls passing rocks, and many asteroid orbits come close, so some hit. A large impact is thought to happen about every 130 000 years. Smaller asteroids hit more often; many break apart in the air and never reach the ground.",
]);

addNotes("7", "7.1", [
  "Think of the rice, chicken and avocado bowl in the book.\nKEYWORD: nutrient — a substance found in food that you need to stay healthy.\nThe six types are carbohydrate, fat (and oil), protein, vitamins, minerals and water.\nKEYWORD: food — what you eat. A plate of food can contain several nutrients at once.\nRice is rich in starch (a carbohydrate). After you eat it, the body breaks starch down to glucose, the fuel cells use in respiration.\nFORMULA: starch → glucose → energy in respiration\nChicken is rich in protein for new cells, repair, haemoglobin and antibodies.\nAvocado gives fats and oils: energy plus the material for cell membranes. Fat under the skin also insulates, like the blubber on a seal.",
  "Iodine test for starch: iodine is orange-brown. If starch is there it turns blue-black. Keep foods separate and use a white tile.\nVitamins are needed in small amounts.\nKEYWORD: vitamin A — helps night vision and white blood cells. Sources: carrots, green vegetables, cheese, some fish.\nKEYWORD: vitamin C — healthy skin, blood vessels and gums. Too little causes scurvy (weak, bleeding gums). Sailors on long voyages got scurvy before they had fresh fruit. Sources: citrus fruit, potatoes, berries.\nKEYWORD: vitamin D — helps you absorb calcium for bones and teeth. Made in the skin in sunlight; oily fish is a food source. Too little can cause rickets.\nKEYWORD: calcium — strong bones and teeth. Milk, almonds.\nKEYWORD: iron — makes haemoglobin. Too little causes anaemia: not enough oxygen, so not enough energy. Red meat, green veg, nuts.\nKEYWORD: water — about 90% of body weight in a young child. Chemicals dissolve in it; blood can flow.",
]);

addNotes("7", "7.2", [
  "KEYWORD: diet — the food you eat each day.\nKEYWORD: balanced diet — all six nutrients plus the right amount of energy.\nFORMULA: 1 megajoule (MJ) = 1 000 000 joules\nYou use more energy if you play sport, grow quickly, or have a larger body. A 15-year-old boy often needs more megajoules than an 8-year-old or a less active 50-year-old.\nExample: a growing child needs extra protein. Someone who sits at a desk all day needs less carbohydrate and fat than an athlete. A pregnant woman needs extra protein, iron and calcium.",
  "KEYWORD: fibre — mostly cellulose from plant cell walls. It is not a nutrient because you do not digest it or send it into the blood. It still keeps faeces moving and helps prevent constipation. Cereal, seeds, fruit and vegetables.\nThe food triangle: plenty of rice, bread and pasta (preferably wholegrain); lots of fruit and veg; quite a lot of meat, fish, eggs or pulses; not too many sweets or fried foods.\nToo much sugar: tooth decay and a higher risk of diabetes. Too much fat: extra weight and a strain on joints, heart and organs.",
]);

addNotes("7", "7.3", [
  "You began as one cell. Cells divide and grow.\nKEYWORD: embryo — the early ball of cells that becomes a baby.\nKEYWORD: growth — the body gets bigger because there are more cells. Needs protein (to build cells) and energy (so cells can divide).\nKEYWORD: development — new organs, stronger muscles, a brain that can walk, talk and play. Stages blend: baby, toddler, child, adolescent, adult (about 18–20 years).\nGrowth is not the same as only putting on fat.",
  "Regular exercise uses food energy, strengthens heart and muscle, and can lift mood.\nSmoking harms the smoker and people nearby.\nKEYWORD: nicotine — addictive; narrows blood vessels.\nKEYWORD: tar — sticky mixture; some chemicals can cause cancer, including lung cancer.\nKEYWORD: carbon monoxide — poisonous gas that sticks to haemoglobin so less oxygen is carried.\nKEYWORD: particulates — tiny carbon particles that damage alveoli.\nFORMULA: less oxygen in blood → less respiration → less energy\nSmoking in pregnancy is linked with more babies born at a low birthweight.",
]);

addNotes("7", "7.4", [
  "KEYWORD: skeleton — the bones inside your body. Insects have an exoskeleton on the outside. Bones need calcium and protein.\nKEYWORD: joint — where two bones meet.\nKEYWORD: hinge joint — moves one way, like a door (elbow, knee, finger joints).\nKEYWORD: ball-and-socket joint — a ball fits a cup so the bone can move in a circle (shoulder, hip).\nBones cannot bend themselves.",
  "KEYWORD: muscle — specialised cells that get shorter. That shortening is contraction. Energy from glucose by respiration.\nKEYWORD: tendon — tough cord that joins muscle to bone. It must not stretch, or the pull would be wasted.\nKEYWORD: ligament — holds bones together at a joint.\nKEYWORD: antagonistic muscles — a pair with opposite jobs. Biceps contracts and triceps relaxes to bend the elbow. Triceps contracts and biceps relaxes to straighten it. Muscles can only pull, never push.\nBiceps has two tendons (bi- = two). Triceps has three attachments (tri- = three).",
]);

addNotes("7", "8.1", [
  "KEYWORD: exothermic — energy exits the reaction and goes into the surroundings.\nKEYWORD: surroundings — everything that is not the reacting chemicals: the beaker, the air, your hands, the thermometer, the water.\nYou usually notice heat: the mixture feels warmer and the thermometer climbs. Some reactions also give out light or sound.\nMemory hook: exo = exit. Heat leaves the chemicals.",
  "How to prove it in the lab — do not guess by touching.\n1. Measure the starting temperature.\n2. Mix and stir.\n3. Record the highest temperature.\n4. Calculate the change.\nFORMULA: ΔT = T_final − T_initial\nIf ΔT is positive (21 °C → 48 °C, so ΔT = +27 °C), the reaction is exothermic. Repeat and take a mean. Eye protection; never heat a sealed container.",
  "Everyday examples:\nFORMULA: hydrogen + oxygen → water\nFORMULA: fuel + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water (+ heat + light)\nFORMULA: carbon + oxygen → carbon dioxide\nFORMULA: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water\nNeutralisation (acid + alkali) also warms the solution. Hand warmers oxidise iron on purpose. Respiration is a slow, controlled burn in mitochondria, not a flame.",
  "Chemicals store energy in bonds. In an exothermic reaction, more energy is released when new bonds form than was needed to break the old bonds. The spare energy heats the surroundings.\nNew substances appear (ash, carbon dioxide, a salt). That is a chemical change. Heating water on a stove is not an exothermic reaction — the water is still H₂O.",
  "Exam traps: a temperature rise of the surroundings = exothermic. A temperature fall = endothermic (topic 8.2). Combustion, respiration, neutralisation and many metal + acid reactions are exothermic. Always measure with a thermometer.\nFORMULA: magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide",
]);

addNotes("7", "8.2", [
  "KEYWORD: endothermic reaction — a chemical change that absorbs thermal energy from the surroundings and stores it in chemical bonds.\nThe temperature at the end is lower than at the start.\nMemory hook: endo = enter. Heat goes into the chemicals.\nFORMULA: ΔT = T_final − T_initial\nIf ΔT is negative, the change is endothermic. Measure with a thermometer. Do not stir with the thermometer.\nIn the book practical you add sodium hydrogen carbonate (bicarbonate of soda) to citric acid or lemon juice. The tube fizzes and feels cooler.",
  "FORMULA: sodium hydrogen carbonate + citric acid → sodium citrate + water + carbon dioxide\nKEYWORD: reactant — a starting substance (here, the acid and the bicarbonate).\nKEYWORD: product — a new substance made (sodium citrate, water and carbon dioxide).\nSherbet sweets are the same two dry powders. Your saliva wets them so they react on your tongue. That is why sherbet feels cool and fizzy. The fizz is carbon dioxide gas.",
  "KEYWORD: endothermic process — energy is taken in, but no new substance is made.\nStirring potassium chloride into water makes the beaker cold. The salt has only dissolved: potassium chloride is the solute and water is the solvent.\nIce melting is another endothermic process. Particles in ice sit in rows and only vibrate. They absorb energy, vibrate more, overcome the forces holding them, and slide as liquid water.\nComing out of a pool feels cold because water on your skin evaporates — another endothermic process. Freezing is the opposite: energy is given out, so it is exothermic.",
  "KEYWORD: surroundings — everything that is not the reacting (or changing) chemicals: the beaker, the air, your skin.\nExothermic: thermal energy is given out to the surroundings (boiling water cooling, a campfire, a self-heating can).\nEndothermic: thermal energy is taken in from the surroundings (ice melting, the citric acid reaction).\nFORMULA: calcium oxide + water → calcium hydroxide\nA self-heating can mixes those two chemicals to warm food when you are camping. It can only be used once.\nAn ice pack from the freezer cools an injury because the ice melts (a process). An instant cold pack has two compartments. You burst the water pouch so ammonium nitrate dissolves. The pack gets cold without a freezer.",
]);

addNotes("7", "8.3", [
  "KEYWORD: oxidation — a metal combining with oxygen.\nKEYWORD: metal oxide — the new substance formed when a metal burns or corrodes in air.\nReactive metals such as magnesium and sodium react readily. Gold hardly reacts at all.",
  "FORMULA: magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide\nThe white ash is the oxide. Energy is given out as heat and light, so this is also exothermic.",
]);

addNotes("7", "8.4", [
  "The most reactive metals react with water to make a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas.\nFORMULA: metal + water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen\nPotassium reacts vigorously. Copper does not react with water.",
  "KEYWORD: hydrogen test — a lit splint gives a squeaky pop if the gas is hydrogen.\nNever collect large amounts of hydrogen near a flame.",
]);

addNotes("7", "8.5", [
  "When a metal reacts with a dilute acid you get a salt and hydrogen. Hydrochloric acid makes chlorides.\nFORMULA: zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen\nCopper is used for water pipes because it is low in the reactivity series, so it does not react with water or dilute acids in ordinary use.",
]);

addNotes("7", "9.1", [
  "A magnet attracts magnetic materials. At Year 7 those metals are iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. Copper, aluminium, silver, tin, gold and most plastics are not magnetic.\nKEYWORD: poles — the ends of a magnet, labelled N and S. The force is strongest there.\nKEYWORD: magnetic field — the space around a magnet where its effects can be detected.\nDetect it with a paperclip, iron filings or a compass.",
  "Magnetic field lines are a drawing code, not strings in the air.\nRules: they join opposite poles; arrows point from N to S; they must not touch or cross. Closer lines mean a stronger field. A horseshoe magnet packs a strong field in the gap.",
  "Opposite poles attract. Like poles repel.\nFORMULA: N + S → attract    N + N or S + S → repel\nExam drawing: about eight lines, arrows N → S, never crossing.",
]);

addNotes("7", "9.2", [
  "Earth behaves like a giant bar magnet. The field is made in the liquid outer core (iron and nickel). A compass needle is a tiny magnet that lines up with that field.\nShips still carry magnetic compasses even when they have satnav. Some birds use the field to migrate. Aurorae happen when particles from the Sun are guided toward the poles.",
  "KEYWORD: geographic north — where Earth’s spin axis meets the surface.\nKEYWORD: magnetic north — a different place in the Arctic; it moves about 60 km a year.\nOutside a magnet, field lines go N → S. The magnetic pole near geographic north behaves like a magnetic south pole, which is why the north-seeking end of a compass is attracted there.",
  "Lab check: hang a bar magnet on a string, or magnetise a needle and float it on cork. Both settle north–south.\nKEYWORD: lodestone — magnetite, a naturally occurring magnet. The first compasses used a floating lodestone needle.",
]);

addNotes("7", "9.3", [
  "KEYWORD: magnetic — the material is attracted to a magnet.\nKEYWORD: magnetised — you have turned it into a magnet.\nKEYWORD: permanent magnet — keeps its field; you cannot switch it off.\nKEYWORD: electromagnet — a coil of insulated wire around a core, a cell and a switch. Current magnetises the core. Switch off and it loses most of its magnetism.",
  "Why use an electromagnet? You can pick up and then drop.\n• Scrapyard crane: lifts iron and steel; switch off to drop the load.\n• Toaster: holds the tray down, then cuts the current so toast pops up.\n• Fire door: holds the door open until the alarm switches it off.\n• Electric bell, motors, and MRI scanners all use electromagnets.",
]);

addNotes("7", "9.4", [
  "Three factors change how strong an electromagnet is.\n1. Number of turns — more turns, stronger.\n2. Current — more cells, stronger.\n3. Core material — iron or soft iron works well; wood, rubber, aluminium and air are weak.\nKEYWORD: soft iron — easy to magnetise and easy to demagnetise (not squashy like clay).\nSchool measurement: how many paperclips it can hold.",
  "KEYWORD: fair test — change only one factor. If turns is the independent variable, control core, current, clip size and counting method.\nNumber of turns is a number, so a line graph. Core material is a category, so a bar chart.\nFORMULA: more turns or more current → more paperclips\nA scrapyard magnet must be far stronger than the one in a toaster.",
]);
